  "overview": {
    "text": "Archaea are a domain of single-celled prokaryotes—organisms whose cells lack a nucleus—and one of three major branches of life, alongside Bacteria and Eukarya. Though they can look like bacteria, they are distinct: their cell membranes have ether-linked, branched lipids, and their cell walls lack bacterial peptidoglycan. Comparisons of ribosomal RNA helped Carl Woese and colleagues identify Archaea as a separate evolutionary lineage, changing how scientists organized the tree of life. Archaea live in ordinary as well as extreme environments. Some, called methanogens, make methane as they obtain energy in oxygen-free conditions, including in some animal digestive systems.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/1-3-types-of-microorganisms",
      "https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/4-6-archaea",
      "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10965645/"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
